Quantitative characterization panels:

Here we should do much more to show useful content in an aesthetic and well
readable way. We came up with ideas for some more specific designs. From some
you can generate more varieties and include in the figure only some, and keep the rest as artifacts.

Panel A:
This should be a horizontal barplot with 6 facets and two bars for each y-axis
labels. Y-axis are resources plus one row for Total (entire database). The
x-axes should be counts (number of items) in the database. The 6 facets should
be: Entities, Associations, Interactions, Identifiers, Structures. In each
resource we count how many of these we have; we also quantify how many of those
can be found in any other resource (shared) and how many are unique for the
given resource (unique). The top bar for each label should be a stacked bar of
these two numbers, the unique part should be a darker shade closer to the axis while the shared a lighter shade on top of it. Then the next bar should be major classes of those items; here we have to plan these classes carefully, ideally have 2-5, max 9 classes for each facet;the sizes of these classes should be shown also as a stacked barplot, the classes color coded. For interactions, we can show the interaction types: Signaling, TF-target, Allosteric regulation, Orthosteric regulation, Transport, Ligand-receptor, etc. For Entities, the classes should be major types: proteins/genes, metabolites, lipids, drugs, food compounds, xenobiotics, other entities (CV terms, ontology terms are entities too afaik). For Structures, the classes should be "level of structural specificity": stereospecific, cis-trans specific, only constitution, variable constitution (e.g. glycerolipids with unknown sn positions), and unknown constitution (variable groups), and no structural information at all (if any). For Associations, please explore the possible categories in the database, maybe here we'll find things like subcellular localization, disease or phenotype, etc. For Identifiers, either we could show authoritative vs. foreign or major ID types (HMDB, ChEBI, UniProt, Gene Symbol, ..., and if there are too many small, group them into "Misc").
And one important trick about this figure: in the Total row, we show the total
numbers but the shared is any item which comes from more than one resource and
the unique is the number of items supported by only a single resource.
Considering the number of resources, this figure will be fairly tall, but
likely still fit half page height. This panel should have many color legends,
one for the classes in each facet, these could be under the respective facets,
and a small one for the unique/shared shading. The resources should be ordered
by their size in the first facet, from largest to smallest, hence the Total
should be the top row.

