I believe to work out the plans for all these detailed specifications, you have
to look really deeply into the database schema and contents. You have to
investigate values of many categories and find out how to extract precisely the
information you need. You should look into the database build in omnipath-build
and the pypath.inputs_v2 module to understand what information and how gets
loaded into the database. And you should look into the web app and web API code
in omnipath-present to understand how the database queries look like. You
should also look into the code of omnipath-client, because there we
reconstitute interactions client side from multiple API queries. After looking
into the code, you can interactively investigate the database and its contents.
For many of the above described plots, you have to carefully plan how to join
and filter to extract the required information. You also have to explore the
values of different categories, count their values, make plan about dropping or
collapsing small ones into "Misc" if there are too many. Do your queries for
numeric values (mostly counts) and decide whether these should be on log scale.
In general, try to avoid double counting things because of a one-to-many join
or other reason, everywhere where we show counts the items should be reasonably
unique. You should also explore how to do the operations with the levels of
structural specificity. Maybe you'll find that no required columns or API is
available and you would need to write a ton of custom code. In this case,
please write up an introduction for a development plan that would implement in
a modular, long-term usable way the required database content and API
operations, and we will implement it in a different session before you continue
with your figures. I'm also wondering if we could help your work with Postgres
or RDKit by downloading some relevant skills for you with best practices and
useful patterns in these technologies?

